From 2d4a74db0bb06e75911b66343be3793355fdd7db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh Boyd <boydjd@jbip.net>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:28:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/16] hw/riscv/esp32s31: a stand-in for the Wi-Fi/Bluetooth
 co-processor

NetBSD runs Espressif's Wi-Fi and Bluetooth firmware on the second hart
and talks to it through message rings in PSRAM, with FROM_CPU interrupts
as doorbells.  QEMU has neither the Wi-Fi MAC nor the Bluetooth
controller, so when hart 1 leaves reset, a model plays the firmware's side
of the protocol: an access point whose frames go to '-nic ...,model=espwifi'
and an HCI transport to a chardev (e.g. Google Bumble's controller).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0158d423kzUx8WWBMMEZ95NX
